contentSections.scenario:
The digest worker:

```ts
async function sendDailyDigest(search: SavedSearch) {
  const matches = await findNewMatches(search); // listings not yet in NotifiedMatch
  if (matches.length === 0) return;
  await sendEmail(search.userId, matches);
  await markNotified(search.id, matches.map(m => m.id));
}
```

The scheduler retries a job that hasn't reported success within 30 seconds, in case the worker crashed. For a user with a large search matching many listings, `findNewMatches` plus `sendEmail` sometimes takes longer than 30 seconds, so the scheduler starts a second worker for the same search while the first is still running.

contentSections.body:
The matching-and-alerts feature has a green suite and 94% coverage. A user still gets emailed a listing paying $85,000 against a saved search with a $120,000 floor. You open the test for the salary filter:

```ts
test('excludes listings below minSalaryUsd', () => {
  const db = { getMinSalary: jest.fn().mockReturnValue(120000) };
  const result = passesSalaryFilter(listing, db);
  expect(db.getMinSalary).toHaveBeenCalled();
});
```

The test checks that `getMinSalary` was called. It never checks what `passesSalaryFilter` actually returned. The real function turns out to be:

```ts
function passesSalaryFilter(listing: Listing, db: SalaryDb): boolean {
  db.getMinSalary(); // fetched but never compared against anything
  return listing.salaryUsd !== null;
}
```

Any listing with a salary at all passes, regardless of amount. Coverage counted every line of `passesSalaryFilter` as executed. It was. None of it was ever checked.

